Subject: Sufic Learning

Geoff says:
>I've never read Gurdjieff (nor do I know if that's the correct spelling),
>but I think I recall Shah mentioning him in "The Sufis".

Correct on the spelling! (Give the boy a prize.)  I've read some
of Gurdjieff and I highly recommend the book: _Meetings with 
Remarkable Men_ (by G.I. Gurdjieff).  The book is an autobiographical
account about the 8 or so people who have influenced Gurdjieff in
his early life. You can read it as adventure narrative because the man
traveled widely in the early part of this century through what is now 
southern Russia, Turkey, Afghanistan and saw and experienced a great
many historical changes. Gurdjieff spent his  whole life in the search of 
knowledge and truth (vague terms, I know), trying to find explanations
for things that he observed and the stories convey what it means to live 
"fully". 

The stories about his adventures are a kick.  I still can't decide
if Gurdjieff was the greatest con-man who ever lived or just an honest,
highly intelligent person. In either case, I have a fair amount of 
respect for him (even though some of his later ideas taken up by the 
Sufic "Fourth Way" are really kooky).


