Date: Tue, 28 Dec 1993 10:51:46 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Start-Up VirtSem: Advice to Entrepreneurs in High-Tech

Though this thread is almost dead (Jim), I once wrote some thoughts
down in a long essay which got lost when my Netcom service dumped my
and I couldn't find my auto-saved copy anywhere.

(Please, Netcommers or others, don't send my advice on how vi or pine
saves, etc.)

I keep thinking I've said this, about start-ups and budding
entrepreneurs, because I have the _memory_ of writing the words. Alas,
gone forever.

Here's a condensed version, almost telegraph-style:

* if 25-30 or under and desirous of doing a high-tech start-up, but
lacking an obviously superior idea, then go to work for a high-tech
firm like Silicon Graphics, LSI Logic, whatever. This'll earn you a
hefty salary, expose you to like-minded people, and a good idea may
come to you or to one of your friends.

* young entrepreneurs rarely "get funding" unless they've had contacts
in companies, have an utterly spectacular idea, etc.

* most Valley start-ups I've seen had heavy involvement from middle
managers within companies. Folks with some management experience, some
ability to manage a "P & L" (profit and loss) account within their
companies, etc. I saw at least 20 spin-offs from Intel, including
Xicor ("Ex-Intel Corp."), Seeq, Sequent, Ncube, Convergent
Technologies (now part of Unisys), LSI Logic, Logic Automation, and
even Apple (Mike Markkula, the funder, left Intel in 1974, just as I
was joining).

* most start-ups go for nearly immediate profitability. (Yes, not all
of them make it....but none of them start with the plan of doing "R &
D" for several years....the burn rate of the money stops this, and
evolutionary theory favors quick tests and course corrections over
long-range efforts.) Intel was incorporated in 1967-8, producing its
first chip within a few months, and was selling its first products in
1969. By 1971, only 3 years after operations began, it unveiled the
4004 microprocessor, and was already shipping the world's first
erasable PROMS and MOS RAMS. (IBM had MOS RAMS internally, but Intel
was the first outside seller.)

* don't think that VCs will fund you to play around and figure out
something neat to do---it will never happen.

* you must have a concrete product plan, a real schedule for
introducing a product with real prospects for real sales. (Yes, we've
all seen the "generic" spreadsheet business plan, which always seems
to have "sales of $100 million in the sixth year," but savvy investors
look to what's really likely, not pie-in-the-sky spreadsheet games.)

* since most 25-year-old are unlikely to pull off what I've described
above, hence my recommendation that conventional jobs be taken in
high-tech industries.

* bluntly speaking, as I have said before, the best start-ups come
from essentially ripping-off the groundwork and learning already done
at a previous company or university. I've cited examples of this
before, especially Intel (Fairchild), Apple (Xerox PARC), Sun
(Stanford), Cisco Systems (Stanford again), etc.

* go to a promising high-tech firm, in computers, networking,
satellites, whatever, and do the best job you can, absorbing
everything, getting a rep for doing a good job, making contacts,
running your own P & L center, etc. Then one day the timing will
appear to be right....a wonderful product idea, knowledgeable friends
and coworkers to recruit, and enough experience to convince VCs that
you know what you're doing. Or, it may even be one of your friends and
you'll be one of _his_ (or _her_) cofounders.

* above all, you must _like_ what you're doing, as you may never get
the chance to do a start-up and may work for many years for someone
else. Also, many of the start-ups I've seen came from once-obscure
work that suddenly became interesting from a commercial standpoint.

So, do what you like, do it for a while, then try a start-up if a
great idea appears and funding seems to be available. But don't, as a
youngster starting out in high-tech, think "doing a start-up" is
something that will just happen to you....nobdody's out there handing
out money to fairly bright people who just think they'd like to be
their own boss (Rule #3, when others fund you, _they_ are the boss).

In the case of Wozniak and Jobs, who were indeed around 22-25 when
funded (by Markkula and others), they had already demonstrated the
Apple I at the Homebrew Computer Club in 1976-7 or so (about the time
I saw a Steve Witham letter in Dr. Dobbs) and were very far along in
building the Apple II. The modest funding from Markkula and others did
not pay for several years of Xanadu-like R & D, just for tooling
charges for the PCBs and plastic case, the initial ad campaign, and
limited hiring of staff.

Thus, Apple was selling product within several months of funding. Had
they failed, as so many other companies did at that time, the
investors would've been out the hundred K or less they put into
it...not a multimillion dollar expense.

There _are_ times when it takes 2-3 years to prepare a product for
sale, but these are indeed dangerous investments and require great
care in evaluating them. I just put $62,500 in such an
investment--gulp!--and it may vanish completely. Or it may turn into
quite a bit of money, if the company succeeds. 

But never think that just because you are a bright person who knows
C++ or neural nets, as examples, and you have time on your hands, that
some "angel" out there will pay you a salary and buy you equipment
while you try to think of a product or service you can sell.

That's the harsh reality.

--Tim May


