B >Betsy

I think that you misunderstand the sorts of changes we are in for.  Seeing 
the future is always rough (see SF stories mixing interstellar travel and 
slide rules) and it will get rougher.  The kinds of changes we face will 
carry ideas along with them.

Networks and computers have caused institutional downsizing in the US and 
Europe not because people independently decided to downsize but because 
the technology made that choice the most efficient arrangement.  The 
Nation State and large corporations were formed in the first place because 
of this sort of encounter between people's desires and the technological 
and organizational realities.  Those realities are changing and so the 
institutions will change.

We are in for major institutional change even if not one person adopts one 
new idea.  The ideas that are already in their heads are enough when you 
mix them up with the new technology.  If you are handed  platter full of 
choices, you have those choices whether you make the choices or not.  If 
you are let out of a cage, you are free whether you believe it or not.  

Since networks multiply choices, they reduce controls.  The effect of 
connecting 5 billion people in peer to peer packet switched networks 
multiplies the number of possible interactions beyond any hope of control. 
 Governments had a hard enough time ruling us when we were bound to the 
soil.  Soon they will not even be able to comprehend the scope of human 
society much less rule it.  

If the KGB and the Stasi couldn't prevent change, what chance do the 
Fibbies have?  They can't double in size as rapidly as markets can.  Note 
that as the web grows, the rulers will have a hard time even passing their 
commands to us (aren't kill files great) much less enforcing those 
commands.  

